{
  "result": "Last autumn, I found a small tin box behind the library shelves, filled with letters nobody had claimed in fifty years. They were not important letters, not really, but they were careful and kind and full of small observations about weather, bread, and the cat who sat on the windowsill every morning at seven. I read them slowly, one per evening, with tea getting cold beside me, and I began to recognize the handwriting as mine, though I had never written them. Perhaps that is what memory does when it grows tired of being accurate: it borrows a gentler voice, tidies the edges, and returns the past to us as if it were news. None of the letters mentioned anything dramatic. No wars, no arrivals by train, no secret promises kept in the rain. Instead, there was a recipe for plum jam, a sketch of a hill I do not remember climbing, and a list of names of birds heard but never seen. And yet I could not stop reading them. Each one made the room seem larger, as if someone had opened a window in a wall I thought was solid. By the final letter, the box was empty but the shelves were not. I put the letters back, closed the lid, and left them for whoever finds them next, hoping they will read them just as slowly, and recognize, with a little shock and gratitude, a life they almost lived, written in a handwriting they almost remember. Indeed.",
